---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('omnisend')!;
const b = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/omnisend-vs-postscript');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/omnisend-vs-postscript');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Postscript distinguish cart from checkout?",
    "answer": "Yes. Its automation guidance lists Product Added to Cart and Checkout Started separately, with later-stage events used to cancel earlier recovery."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should Postscript replace the email sender?",
    "answer": "This comparison evaluates adding or moving SMS while retaining email. Assign email and SMS owners explicitly and verify the actual handoff; do not assume a shared customer ID coordinates sends."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do I know whether another SMS platform is worth it?",
    "answer": "Compare retained-email recovery with retained email plus the proposed SMS program in an eligible cohort, including incremental order value, discounts, message charges and operating work."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every SMS carry the email discount?",
    "answer": "No. Fix checkout friction first, preserve offer exclusions and test stacking. A second channel does not justify a second incentive."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Decide whether SMS should stay inside Omnisend or earn a separate place alongside your existing email recovery.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Omnisend", "reason": "Manage recovery email and SMS steps together when one team owns both channels."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Postscript", "reason": "Evaluate a dedicated SMS recovery program alongside the email sender you intend to retain."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>The purchase decision is about the next channel dollar</h2>
<p>If Omnisend email already recovers eligible checkouts, adding Postscript should solve a specific SMS operating need. Compare keeping SMS within Omnisend with assigning SMS to Postscript while retaining email. Name the person responsible for both send calendars, exclusions, offers and order reconciliation. A separate sender introduces a handoff to maintain, so its additional value must cover the message bill and that work. This page does not report a measured winner.</p>
<h2>Match the recovery stage before comparing reach</h2>
<p><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/6659889-abandoned-cart-and-abandoned-checkout-automations">Omnisend’s abandonment guide</a> distinguishes identified cart and checkout contacts and supports recovery links in email, SMS and push. Its abandoned-products display belongs in email; the cart-return URL is a separate link. It also notes that removed products are not tracked. Use the checkout as the final basket review rather than promising every reminder image reflects the current selection.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564025-understanding-infinity-testing-in-automation-flows">Postscript’s automation guidance</a> lists Product Added to Cart and Checkout Started as separate triggers. It recommends canceling cart recovery at checkout or order creation, and checkout recovery at order creation. It also specifies different cart and checkout link tags. Those trigger and cancellation mechanics are relevant here; the premium AI testing feature described by that guide is not a requirement or evidence of better recovery. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Write the sender contract</h2>
<p>For every stage, write down the trigger, eligible subscription state, intended sender, message delay and cancellation event. If Postscript owns SMS, disable equivalent Omnisend SMS steps for the same cohort. Keep the retained email flow only where it serves the intended sequence. Disable duplicate Shopify recovery messages when replacing them. A shared purchase feed should cancel both channels, but it does not by itself prevent two pre-purchase reminders arriving together.</p>
<p>Decide how a recent email delivery changes SMS eligibility and how a recent SMS changes the next email. Verify what your actual integration exposes and how quickly it arrives. Do not assume a native Omnisend–Postscript synchronization contract. If dependable cross-provider message history is unavailable, use deliberately separated cohorts or assign a single provider to the whole recovery sequence instead of building a fragile send-time race.</p>
<h2>Proposed checks for the handoff</h2>
<p>Use email-only, SMS-only, dual-subscribed and opted-out test contacts. A checkout phone number alone does not establish SMS marketing permission. Preserve subscription source and opt-out state during any move, and verify the approved sender identity and operational setup before sending. Check quiet-hour deferrals: a delayed SMS should re-check whether the shopper purchased while waiting, rather than arriving after the receipt.</p>
<p>Add to cart, start checkout while the first reminder waits, and place an order immediately before the next planned send. Log events, cancellations, provider send decisions and actual deliveries. Repeat with a second checkout and duplicate event delivery. Follow each trigger-specific return link on mobile, including the in-app browser. This exercise must demonstrate one intended recovery path; it is not a claim that either supplier passed testing on your store.</p>
<h2>Make SMS earn a different job from email</h2>
<p>For a bundle discount that failed to apply, email can explain eligibility and show the bundle; a short SMS can point to help only after the price rule is corrected. For BNPL or financing friction, provide a payment-help route without promising approval. For a B2B cart below minimum quantity, explain the required pack or quantity rather than sending a generic urgency text.</p>
<p>A repeat buyer with a saved address may need only a return link. International shipping uncertainty requires a quote and duty explanation, not a universal shipping promise. A subscription toggle needs an edit path and clear purchase choices. New mobile shoppers need a checkout that opens correctly; sending more reminders cannot repair a broken link.</p>
<p>For unavailable sizes, pause the original offer and provide a suitable stock-alert or alternative path. For gifts, provide sizing, gift-note editing and delivery-cutoff information you can substantiate. Test welcome and recovery discounts against bundle and sale exclusions so both senders cannot accidentally stack offers. A refund should reduce reported net sales and route to support; it is not a reason to resume the original abandoned checkout.</p>
<h2>Budget discounts and channels separately</h2>
<p>The original hypothetical $791 basket at 63% gross margin produces $498.33 gross profit before incentives at fixed goods costs. A 15% code costs $118.65 and leaves $379.68. The second $580 basket at 37% margin yields $214.60; an 18% code costs $104.40 and leaves $110.20. These are illustrative gross-profit calculations, excluding fulfillment, payment fees, returns, platform and messaging charges. The discount percentages are not recommended limits.</p>
<p>Request comparable quotes for your contact count, destination mix, message volume and intended message format. Include platform minimums, applicable carrier charges, sender setup or migration work, additional integrations and the staff time needed to reconcile two systems. If email already stays in Omnisend, do not count its entire subscription as a saving from adding Postscript. Compare the extra cost of each SMS option against its extra benefit.</p>
<h2>Measure the marginal SMS decision</h2>
<p>For dual-eligible shoppers, a proposed randomized comparison can keep the existing email sequence stable and vary the added SMS program. Size the exercise for traffic and expected order rate, keep assignment stable across cart and checkout, and include opt-outs and support burden. If comparing two SMS providers, hold audience rules and the commercial offer constant enough to explain the difference. These are proposed evaluations, not previously observed results.</p>
<p>Deduplicate Shopify order IDs across dashboards and report net sales after discounts, cancellations and refunds before estimating profit after delivery and channel costs. Credited revenue from two vendors must not be added together. Prefer one shared sender when operational simplicity outweighs the demonstrated incremental gain; prefer a separate SMS owner only when its verified workflow and economics justify the handoff.</p>
<h2>Move SMS without leaving two active senders</h2>
<p>Before cutover, reconcile subscribed numbers, suppressions, active automations and scheduled messages. Name the operator who pauses the old SMS queues and the operator who validates purchase cancellation in the replacement. Keep email ownership explicit throughout. If subscription state, sender setup or cancellation fails, pause new SMS enrollment, reconcile pending messages and restore the previous owner only after duplicate sends are ruled out.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
